## Trip search process (repeat for every new trip)

1. Get from Chris: destination(s) under consideration, date range (or a fixed
   window), trip length, budget, and any trip-specific must-haves (e.g. "3 days of
   ski lessons," "beginner-friendly").
2. If the date range spans multiple candidate weeks, live-price flights (Kiwi.com)
   and lodging (Booking.com) for each candidate week in parallel, in the family's
   real passenger mix computed from birthdates above — pick the cheapest combined
   week unless another week has a hard reason to win (e.g. a birthday, snow
   conditions, school holidays).
3. Itemize the full budget, not just flights+lodging: activity-specific costs
   (lessons, passes, rentals), local transfer, and a meals/groceries estimate
   belong in the same table. Flag clearly whether the total is within the given
   budget or by how much it's over.
4. Where live pricing isn't available for a category (e.g. Pamporovo lift passes
   and ski school had no connector coverage), say so explicitly and mark those
   numbers as published-rate estimates, not live quotes — don't blur the two
   together.
5. If over budget, give concrete levers to close the gap (fewer lesson days,
   skip rental for gear already owned, tighter grocery budget, earlier booking for
   pre-sale discounts) rather than just restating the shortfall.
6. Write the trip up as its own file under `trips/` (see below) so this FAMILY.md
   stays purely reusable and each trip's specifics stay contained.

